A||N||8||Hoostine||building a v8 for my fiero||||1||
Z||000000||Hoostine||07-15-2001||03:15 AM||||so i've got this 400 block and heads, but i've got a few questions for you guys who've done a few v8 conversions.<P>i'm thinking i should build the motor to be a high revving hi hp, low tq motor.  i mean, low torque on a 400 sbc is still GOBS of torque.  well, compared to the 2.8 in my fiero now.  and besides, i don't need to pick up the front tires when i take off, even tho that'd be cool.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>anyways, i can always put on n2o for the midrange power boost later if i think i need more.  but i don't think i will (*need* that is, *want* however...)<P>is there a good reason to have more than 350 lbft or so of tq on such a small car?  i don't think so.  and this way, it'll put less stress on my 4 spd and i'll have a higher redline which means higher top speed.<P>i'm thinking short runner intake, long duration cam, some headwork, dual valve springs, roller hydraulic valve train (will these fit in a 400sbc?), forged bottom end, 9.6-10:1 cr, forged aluminum pistons (do they get brittle and crack like al rods do?), long rods/short pistons. <BR>hopefully 6500+ redline(hey, honda's do it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>what kind of frame reinforcing is needed to handle big torque, or big hp?  i've never driven a fiero, much less taken one apart.  i just got mine yesterday and i haven't even gotten it to my house yet.<P>the car will eventually have the fino body on it with the finale nose, some nice suspension, new interior, basically a WHOLE new car(the tranny and frame will remain  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>--Hoostine<BR>p.s. i'm building the engine over a span of about a year.  i have another 400 that's going in the fiero to keep me from rushing the first  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>i was thinking of de-stroking it, but i've decided not to...<P>i wish someone would make a v8 kit to mount a mopar engine in, i like them much more than chevy's (dons asbestos suit)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||07-15-2001||06:53 AM||||good luck with it and welcome. i'd like to try a v8 build myself (2 actually, probay won't get even one done). hope the more knowledgable folks give you good answers and i'll peek over your shoulder and take notes.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>SERENITY NOW!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||gtcash||07-15-2001||08:13 AM||||Welcome to the forum,<BR>I have a V8 Fiero...they are awesome!!!<BR>I think the main questions you have to ask yourself is: Is it a daily driver?  Show Car?   or Race car?<BR>You will build your V8 accordingly.<BR>Be sure to check out Archie's V8 web site.<BR>"The Mayor"||||reg||6
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||07-15-2001||08:46 AM||||Hey Hoostine!<BR>Good luck in your endevor!<BR>I have built up a few sbc 350's in the past and I am working on 2 right now for other people...<BR>Personally, I am building up a 3800 for my Fiero, as I feel the V8 is too big for the engine compartment, where a V6 will fit with no major mods...<P>You should be able to get 7000 RPM out of a well built sbc and have it be a reliable daily driver...<P>The formula I use for such a motor is, flat top 9.5:1 pistons, Crane cam with hydrolic lifters, Victor jr manifold with 2 inch spacer and a Holly 650 vac secondaries...<BR>Use TRW Clevite 77 bearings and Chrome molly rings and assemble with plenty of assembly lube...<BR>Don't bother with the 2.02 intake heads, as the 1.94 intakes breathe well enough for this aplication...<BR>I don't have the model number for the cam, but it was a bit rumppy, not quite .4 inch lift and about 280 deg. duration...<P>I had an engine built to these specs in my 63 Nova, and I drove to the track and raced on saturdays, then drove it to work on monday...<BR>It lasted 5 years till the oil pump shaft sheared...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||07-15-2001||09:24 AM||||I don't know about aftermarket rods, but with stock rods a 400 is <I><B>NOT</I></B> a revving engine: 5,000 max.<BR>The reason: to fit all that stroke, the rods had to be relieved slightly at the big end to clear the block. When 400 rods break, they do so at a lower RPM than 350 rods, and break at the big end, rather than at the small end like 350 rods do.<P>If you want to do something awesome with that 400 block, read this article: <A HREF="http://www.airflowresearch.com/Articles/A3-P1.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.airflowresearch.com/Articles/A3-P1.htm</A>  <P>It's about a long rod/short stroke 350 built with a 400 block and a 327 crank. It used a 215 degree cam and made over 400 HP.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||07-15-2001||01:38 PM||||My first thought was to de-stroke it,use a 300 degree cam,single plane manifold(Victor team whatever).<BR>We had a car that used a 400 stock bottom end with a tunnel ram,310 degree cam,it was the valve train that had began to fail,and it was all of the best stainless stuff.<BR>The stroke of a 327 gives a real nice sound that's for sure.||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||07-15-2001||04:07 PM||||Definately de-stroke it ;)<P>Your intuitions are correct, you do not need 400+lb-ft of torque at 18000rpm in a fiero!!<P>However, you WILL be able to make use of an all rooler, all forged  motor that winds to 7000rpm!! ":)<P>Using the 327 crank, and large rods (check that link, the rod ratio is important! :)<BR>you will have a VERY powerful engine that actually likes to rev, and is perfect for the car!<P>ps: Welcome to the fourm, and enjoy!<BR>(and we all want to hear ALL about this car!!! :)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000007||Archie||07-15-2001||06:20 PM||||Please remember that when you use a 400 Crank, you need to have the F/W & HBP (that come with our kit) balanced with the 400 crank.<P>Archie<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Hoostine||07-15-2001||11:53 PM||||I read that article shortly after posting the message and the idea was bouncing around my head quite a bit.  since the 400 block is JUST a block (and heads) i think that'd be the best way to go.  besides, then i'd have something truly unique.  a 350, but in a 400 block, in a fiero...how many people have that?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>wh00, high revving v8 power, here i come!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>i guess i'm gonna have to show a few s2000's and nsx's how it's done eh?<P>--Hoostine<P>ps archie, i know all about that crap on the 400...it's annoying.  thanx for the info tho!<P>pps i showed my friends who helped me haul my new fiero home today the finale and fino body kits which *immediately* shut up their crys of "why do you want this piece of crap?!?" and made them say "i want one too"<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
